    "answer": {
      "short": "$(0,-3)$ and $(4,5)$.\n\nIntersect the perpendicular bisector of $AB$ with the radius condition.",
      "worked": "The midpoint of $AB$ is $M(2,1)$, and its gradient is\n$$\\begin{align*}m_{AB}&=\\frac{0-2}{4-0}\\\\m_{AB}&=-\\frac12.\\end{align*}$$\nThe perpendicular bisector has gradient $2$ and passes through $M$:\n$$\\begin{align*}y-1&=2(x-2)\\\\y&=2x-3.\\end{align*}$$\nLet the centre be $(x,y)$. Its distance from $B(4,0)$ is $5$:\n$$\\begin{align*}(x-4)^2+y^2&=25\\\\(x-4)^2+(2x-3)^2&=25\\\\5x^2-20x+25&=25\\\\5x^2-20x&=0\\\\5x(x-4)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $x=0$ or $x=4$. Substituting into $y=2x-3$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=2(0)-3=-3\\\\y&=2(4)-3=5.\\end{align*}$$\nThe two possible centres are $(0,-3)$ and $(4,5)$. Each lies on the perpendicular bisector and is $5$ units from both $A$ and $B$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 142
  },
